From d3c348c97edbbdb7ea1ff7cb2ee35144a538d1cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kettle <kettle@localhost>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 16:33:13 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] asr: add a D3D12 backend for Arm ASR, with DXIL built
 from its HLSL

Arm Accuracy Super Resolution (MIT) is FSR 2.2.2 reworked for mobile GPUs,
but Arm only ships a Vulkan backend. This adds the pieces to run it on D3D12:

- ffxm_dx12.cpp implements ASR's FfxmInterface on D3D12: root signatures
  built from shader reflection, graphics PSOs per render-target format set
  (most ASR passes are full-screen fragment shaders), resource state
  tracking with UAV barriers, initial-data uploads, clears and copies. It
  depends only on d3d12.h and ASR's host headers.
- gen_asr_shaders.py compiles ASR's own HLSL to DXIL (SM6.0, min16float,
  which vkd3d-proton lowers to native fp16 where the device has it) with a
  Linux DXC, deduplicating permutations by preprocessed source (240 -> 100
  blobs, ~0.9 MiB), and emits the binding reflection the backend needs.
  Only the passes OptiScaler schedules are built.
- build.sh fetches ASR at tag 25.06, builds its host library as
  libasr_arm64ec.a, and runs the generator when $DXC passes a probe
  compile; without one the DLL still builds and ASR reports unavailable.

Checked on a desktop GPU under wine + vkd3d-proton against ASR's own Vulkan
backend on a synthetic jittered scene: PSNR matches within 0.1 dB for every
preset, static and panning, low- and display-resolution motion vectors.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
